t-up from E (above x-axis)\n- Back to A(0,0): left-down from M\n\nConnecting these sequentially, does this trace the outer boundary without crossing? From A to F to N: curves downward to the left, then up to B, up to C, left to E, up to M, down to A. It should form a simple heptagon, no self-intersections.\n\nTo apply shoelace, we need to traverse the boundary consistently (all turns left or all right). Let's check the order by computing the cross product of consecutive edge vectors to see orientation.\n\nEdges as vectors:\n\nFrom A to F: F - A = (f_x, 6) [f_x=c_x/7 >0]\n\nFrom F to N: N - F = (40 - g_x - f_x, -30 - 6) = (40 - 6f_x, -36) [since g_x=5f_x, because g_x=5c_x/7=5*(7f_x)=5f_x, yes! Wait, f_x = c_x/7 ⇒ c_x=7f_x ⇒ g_x=5c_x/7=5f_x. Oh my goodness, this is a key simplification I missed earlier!\n\nSince F is (f_x, 6), and G is (5f_x, 30) (because g_x=5c_x/7=5*(7f_x)=5f_x, and y=5*6=30), that's much simpler. Why did I not see this before? Because when we said F=(c_x/7, 6), so c_x=7f_x, hence G=(5c_x/7,30)=(5f_x,30). Yes! Let's reset all coordinates using f_x instead of c_x, since c_x=7f_x, which simplifies things significantly.\n\nLet's restart coordinate assignments with this realization:\n\nLet’s define t = c_x/7, so c_x = 7t, therefore:\n\n- C = (7t, 42) [since c_y=42, AC=√((7t)² + 42²)=√(49t² + 1764)=7√(t² + 36)=91 ⇒ wait, no! Wait, AC=91, so √((7t)² + 42²)=91 ⇒ divide both sides by 7: √(t² + 36)=13 ⇒ t² + 36=169 ⇒ t²=133 ⇒ t=√133. Wait, hold on! Is this necessary? Wait, no, actually, when we computed the area of DEGF, we got c_y=42, but AC length is fixed at 91, so yes, but do we need the actual value of t? Maybe not, because areas might depend only on heights or linear combinations where t cancels.\n\nWait, but let's formalize this substitution because it's cleaner:\n\nLet’s let s = c_x / 7, so that since F = (c_x / 7, 6) = (s, 6), where s is the x-coordinate of F, which is a scalar we can treat as a variable, but constrained by AC=91.\n\nSince AC=91, A(