=588. Is the heptagon area equal to the area of triangle ABC?\n\nWait, but let's sanity check with the approximate coordinates to see if this makes sense. Total area of ABC is 588, as we found (base AB=28, height 42, area 588). If the heptagon has the same area, that suggests that the heptagon encloses the entire triangle ABC plus some external regions? Wait, no—if area is same, then the external regions must cancel out in area?\n\nWait, let's take the approximate values we had earlier (s≈11.5, so s=√133≈11.5):\n\nCompute coordinates:\n\nA=(0,0), F=(11.5,6), N=(-17.5,-30), B=(28,0), C=(80.5,42), E=(20,0), M=(19,12)\n\nNow compute shoelace manually with these numbers to check:\n\nPoints in order:\n\nA(0,0), F(11.5,6), N(-17.5,-30), B(28,0), C(80.5,42), E(20,0), M(19,12), A(0,0)\n\nCompute S1 = sum xi yi+1:\n\n0*6 + 11.5*(-30) + (-17.5)*0 + 28*42 + 80.5*0 + 20*12 + 19*0 =\n\n0 - 345 + 0 + 1176 + 0 + 240 + 0 = (-345 + 1176) = 831; 831 + 240 = 1071\n\nS2 = sum yi xi+1:\n\n0*11.5 + 6*(-17.5) + (-30)*28 + 0*80.5 + 42*20 + 0*19 + 12*0 =\n\n0 - 105 - 840 + 0 + 840 + 0 + 0 = (-105 -840 +840) = -105\n\nS1 - S2 = 1071 - (-105) = 1176\n\nArea = 1/2 * |1176| = 588. Yes! Exactly, same result, and notice that the s terms canceled, even though we plugged in s≈11.5, the net effect was 1176.\n\nWait, but why is the area of the heptagon equal to the area of triangle ABC? That seems surprising at first glance, but let's see what's happening geometrically.\n\nThe heptagon AFNBCEM: let's see which regions it includes. Triangle ABC is inside the heptagon? Let's check if the entire triangle ABC is contained within AFNBCEM.\n\nTake a point inside ABC, say centroid: ( (0+28+7s)/3, (0+0+42)/3 ) ≈ ( (0+28+80.5)/3, 14 ) ≈ (108.5/3,14)≈(36.17,14). Is this inside AFNBCEM? Let's see the boundaries: AF is from A to F (inside ABC), BC is the side, CE is from C to E (on AB), EM is from E to M (outside?), BN is from B to N (outside?), so maybe the heptagon wraps around ABC?\n\nWait, alternatively, think of the heptagon as composed of triangle ABC plus two \"extensions\" minus some overlaps? But in our shoelace, we got exactly the area of ABC, which is interesting.\n\nWait, let's test with a different configuration. Suppose we take s very small, but wait, s can't be too small because AC=91, s=√133≈11.5, so it's fixed. But in our calculation, s canceled out, meaning the area is constant regardless of the triangle's shape (as long as the segment lengths and quadrilateral area conditions are satisfied), which they are (quadrilateral area fixed implies c_y=42, hence area ABC=588 fixed, and s is determined by AC=91, so s is fixed, but in reality, the problem gives specific segment lengths on AB and AC, so the triangle is uniquely determined up to similarity? Wait, no—AB=28, AC=91 fixed, so triangle is determined up to angle at A, but we fixed the area of DEGF, which fixed the height, hence fixed the angle? Wait, no: area of DEGF depended only on the height (c_y), so yes, the height is fixed, so area ABC is fixed, and the base AB and AC are fixed, so the triangle is fixed up to the angle, but actually, no—if AB and AC are fixed lengths, and the height is fixed, then the angle at A is fixed (since height = AC sin A = 42, AC=91, so sin A=42/91=6/13, fixed, so angle A is fixed). Therefore, the triangle is uniquely determined, but our shoelace showed that the heptagon area equals ABC area, independent of s (which is determined by AC and height, so s is fixed), but in the algebra, s canceled, so even if s changed (but it's fixed), the area still comes out the same.\n\nWait, but in the shoelace calculation, we derived that S1 - S2=1176 always, for any s, so even if the triangle were different (but with the given conditions), the area would still be 588. But in this case, the conditions fix the height, so area ABC=588, and the heptagon happens to have the same area.\n\nBut let's verify with another method to be safe, maybe by subtracting areas from a larger figure or adding a