(1)=8. Yep, 8 subsets. Good, let's check another way: total number of 4-element subsets of 1-8 is C(8,4)=70, but we know only 8 sum to 18, seems reasonable.\n\nNow, for each subset A (size 4, sum 18), the complement B is also size 4, sum 18 (since total sum 36). Now, we need to consider the positions: A corresponds to odd positions (1,3,5,7), B to even positions (2,4,6,8). We need the number of permutations where the number is divisible by 2, i.e., the last digit (position 8) is even. Position 8 is in B (even positions), so d₈ ∈ B, and d₈ must be even (i.e., in E={2,4,6,8}).\n\nTherefore, for each subset A, let's define:\n\n- B = complement of A\n- Let t = number of even digits in B (i.e., |B ∩ E|)\n- Then, as discussed earlier, for each subset A, the number of valid permutations is t * 4! * 3!, because:\n  - Choose an even digit from B for position 8 (t choices)\n  - Permute the remaining 3 even digits in B into positions 2,4,6 (3! ways)\n  - Permute all 4 digits in A into positions 1,3,5,7 (4! ways)\n\nPerfect, so now we need, for each of the 8 subsets A, compute t = |B ∩ E| = |E \\ (A ∩ E)| = 4 - |A ∩ E|, since E has 4 elements. Wait, E is the set of even digits, size 4, so |E \\ X| = 4 - |X| for any X ⊆ E.\n\nBut |A ∩ E| = x, which was defined earlier for each subcase when we considered A. Let's go back to each subcase and compute x=|A∩E|, hence t=4−x.\n\nFrom earlier breakdown:\n\nSubcase1: A={2,4,5,7}; A∩E={2,4} (since E={2,4,6,8}), so x=2 ⇒ t=4−2=2\n\nSubcase2: A={2,6,3,7}; A∩E={2,6}, x=2 ⇒ t=2\n\nSubcase3: Four subsets, let's take each one by one:\n\n- {2,8,1,7}: A∩E={2,8}, x=2 ⇒ t=2\n- {2,8,3,5}: A∩E={2,8}, x=2 ⇒ t=2\n- {4,6,1,7}: A∩E={4,6}, x=2 ⇒ t=2\n- {4,6,3,5}: A∩E={4,6}, x=2 ⇒ t=2\n\nAll four subsets in Subcase3 have x=2, so t=2 each.\n\nSubcase4: A={4,8,1,5}; A∩E={4,8}, x=2 ⇒ t=2\n\nSubcase5: A={6,8,1,3}; A∩E={6,8}, x=2 ⇒ t=2\n\nWait a second! Hold on, in every subcase, x=|A∩E|=2? Let's confirm with the examples:\n\nSubcase1: A={2,4,5,7} – evens: 2,4 (yes, two evens), odds:5,7 (two odds)\n\nSubcase2: A={2,6,3,7} – evens:2,6 (two evens)\n\nSubcase3: All four subsets in Subcase3: {2,8,1,7} has 2,8 (evens); {2,8,3,5} has 2,8; {4,6,1,7} has 4,6; {4,6,3,5} has 4,6 – all two evens each.\n\nSubcase4: {4,8,1,5} – 4,8 (two evens)\n\nSubcase5: {6,8,1,3} – 6,8 (two evens)\n\nOh my goodness! Every subset A with sum 18 has exactly two even digits? Let's check why that happened in our cases. When x=2 (we found only x=2 possible), so yes, in all valid subsets, |A∩E|=2, therefore |B∩E|=4−2=2 for every subset A.\n\nIs that true? Let's verify with another example, say, take Subset1: A={2,4,5,7}, B={1,3,6,8}. Even digits in B: 6,8 (two evens), correct.\n\nSubset2: A={2,6,3,7}, B={1,4,5,8} – evens in B: 4,8 (two evens)\n\nSubset3 first one: A={2,8,1,7}, B={3,4,5,6} – evens in B: 4,6 (two evens)\n\nSecond Subset3: A={2,8,3,5}, B={1,4,6,7} – evens in B: 4,6 (two evens)\n\nThird Subset3: A={4,6,1,7}, B={2,3,5,8} – evens in B: 2,8 (two evens)\n\nFourth Subset3: A={4,6,3,5}, B={1,2,7,8} – evens in B: 2,8 (two evens)\n\nSubset4: A={4,8,1,5}, B={2,3,6,7} – evens in B: 2,6 (two evens)\n\nSubset5: A={6,8,1,3}, B={2,4,5,7} – evens in B: 2,4 (two evens)\n\nWow, okay, so in every single case, the complement B also contains exactly two even digits. That's surprising, but mathematically, since we forced A to have two even digits (as x=2 was necessary), and E has four elements, so B gets the other two evens, hence B has two even digits too.\n\nWait, let's recap why x had to be 2: when we considered the sums, for sum 18 with four digits, splitting into evens and odds: sum of evens (x elements) plus sum of odds (4−x elements) =18. Sum of all evens=20, sum of all odds=16. If x=2, sum of two evens + sum of two odds=18, which worked out (as we saw, leading to 8 subsets). If x=1, sum of one even + three odds=18, but one even is even, three odds sum to odd, so total odd≠18 (even), impossible. x=3: sum of three evens + one odd=18, three evens sum to even